 (out of 9) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|:---:|\n| Gas welding / oxy-acetylene / 3 flame types | **8** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| TIG vs MIG comparison | 4 | 4M | Comparison table | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Electrode coatings / purpose | 4 | 3-4M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Polarity / power sources (AC vs DC) | 4 | 3-4M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Welding defects (causes & remedies) | 3 | 4-7M | Theory + Table | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Classification of welding processes | 3 | 3-4M | Theory + Chart | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| MIG welding (detailed with sketch) | 2 | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| TIG welding (detailed with sketch) | 1 | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| Electrode coding system | 1 | 4M | Theory | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| ⚠️ Brazing vs Soldering | 5 | 3M | Comparison | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Welding joints (butt, lap, T, etc.) | 2 | 4M | Theory + Diagram | ⚠️ Borderline |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Gas welding / oxy-acetylene / flame types is the SINGLE MOST ASKED TOPIC across all 9 papers — 8 out of 9.** This is not a suggestion. If you study one thing from Chapter 2, this is it. Know the 3 flame types (neutral, oxidizing, carburizing), their temperature zones, which metals each is used for, and the oxy-acetylene equipment setup. Be able to draw all 3 from memory.\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 3 — Forming and Shaping Processes (CO-3)\n\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 9) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|:---:|\n| Extrusion (forward vs backward) | **7** | 4-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Hot working vs Cold working | **6** | 3-7M | Comparison table | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Rolling (principle, operations, mill types) | **6** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Wire drawing vs Tube drawing | **5** | 4-7M | Theory + Diagram + Comparison | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Strain hardening (effects, concept) | 2 | 3M | Theory | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| ⚠️ Ingot / Bloom / Billet / Slab definitions | 4 | 3M | Definitions | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Rolling formula l = √(RΔt) | 1 | 4M | Derivation | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Forward/backward slip, neutral point | 1 | 3M | Theory | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Recrystallization temperature | 1 | 4M | Theory | ⚠️ Borderline |\n\n---\n\n## 4. 🎯 Strategy A: 20/30 Marks (~6-8 hours)\n\nStudy these in this exact order. Tier 1 topics only. If you run out of time, stop — you've covered the highest-probability material.\n\n| # | Topic | Chapter | Est. Time | What to learn | Expected marks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Gas welding + 3 flame types | Ch2 | 1 hr | Oxy-acetylene setup diagram, neutral/oxidizing/carburizing flame sketches + which metals each welds. Gas cutting process. | 3-7M |\n| 2 | Types of patterns | Ch1 | 30 min | All pattern types (solid, split, match plate, cope & drag, gated, sweep, skeleton, follow board, loose piece). Sketch each. | 3-4M |\n| 3 | Pattern allowances | Ch1 | 45 min | Shrinkage, machining, draft, shake, distortion allowances. What each is, why needed. | 3-7M |\n| 4 | Hot working vs Cold working | Ch3 | 30 min | Comparison table: temperature, grain size, surface finish, strength, ductility, residual stress, dimensional accuracy. | 3-7M |\n| 5 | Extrusion (forward vs backward) | Ch3 | 45 min | Forward extrusion sketch + process. Backward extrusion sketch + process. Comparison table. Hydrostatic extrusion (brief). | 4-7M |\n| 6 | Investment casting | Ch1 | 30 min | Full step-by-step process with sketch (wax pattern → ceramic coating → dewaxing → pouring → knockout). Advantages/applications. | 7M |\n| 7 | Wire drawing vs Tube drawing | Ch3 | 45 min | Both processes with sketches. Die angles, reduction ratio. Comparison table. | 4-7M |\n| 8 | Rolling (principle + mills + roll pass) | Ch3 | 1.5 hr | Rolling principle sketch, types of mills (2-high, 3-high, 4-high, cluster, tandem). Roll pass sequence. Products (ingot→bloom→billet→slab). | 4-7M |\n\n**Total study time: ~6.5 hours**\n**Expected marks: 20-24/30**\n\n### DO NOT WASTE TIME ON (from old PYQs):\n\n| ❌ Topic | Why students waste time on it | Old PYQ frequency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resistance welding (Spot/Seam) | Looks like it belongs in welding chapter | 5/9 papers |\n| Forging (all types + operations) | Heavy PYQ history, but NOT in your syllabus | 7/9 papers |\n| Sheet metal (blanking/piercing/shearing) | Very frequently asked | 8/9 papers |\n| Injection moulding / Plastics | In almost every paper | 9/9 papers |\n| Super finishing (lapping/honing/burnishing) | In almost every paper | 8/9 papers |\n| Laser/Plasma/Electron beam welding | Looks like welding chapter content | 4/9 papers |\n| Manufacturing process classification | Was Q1 in most old papers | 7/9 papers |\n| Chvorinov's rule / Riser design | Looks like casting topic | 3/9 papers |\n| Cupola furnace | Looks like foundry topic | 2/9 papers |\n\n---\n\n## 5. 🎯 Strategy B: 25/30 Marks (~12-15 hours)\n\nEverything from Strategy A (6.5 hours), PLUS:\n\n| # | Topic | Chapter | Est. Time | Why study | Expected marks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 9 | TIG vs MIG comparison | Ch2 | 30 min | 4/9 papers. Know process differences, shielding gas, filler wire, applications, current type. | 4M |\n| 10 | Welding defects (causes + remedies) | Ch2 | 45 min | 3/9 papers. Porosity, undercut, spatter, incomplete fusion, slag inclusion, cracking. Cause-remedy table. | 4-7M |\n| 11 | Electrode coatings + purpose | Ch2 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Quick-win 3M question. Functions: arc stability, gas shielding, slag formation, alloying. | 3M |\n| 12 | Polarity (straight/reverse) + AC vs DC | Ch2 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Quick-win 3M question. Which polarity for thick/thin work, heat distribution. | 3-4M |\n| 13 | Moulding sand properties | Ch1 | 30 min | 3/9 papers. Permeability, flowability, collapsibility, cohesiveness, refractoriness, green strength. | 4-7M |\n| 14 | Core types / core making | Ch1 | 30 min | 3/9 papers. Types of cores (horizontal, vertical, balanced, hanging), core prints, chaplets. | 4M |\n| 15 | Sand casting step-by-step procedure | Ch1 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Pattern → moulding → core setting → closing → pouring → solidification → shakeout → cleaning. | 3M |\n| 16 | Classification of welding processes | Ch2 | 20 min | 3/9 papers. Draw the classification tree (fusion vs pressure, gas vs arc vs resistance, etc.) | 3-4M |\n| 17 | Strain hardening (concept + effects) | Ch3 | 15 min | 2/9 papers. What happens to strength/ductility/hardness. Stress-strain curve changes. | 3M |\n| 18 | Shell mould casting | Ch1 | 25 min | 2/9 papers. Full process with sketch. Thermosetting resin bonded sand + heated metal pattern. | 7M |\n| 19 | MIG welding — full process | Ch2 | 25 min | 2/9 papers, but your syllabus lists it explicitly. Know it for a potential 7M question. | 7M |\n| 20 | TIG welding — full process | Ch2 | 25 min | 1/9 papers, but explicitly in syllabus. Setup sketch + non-consumable tungsten electrode + inert gas. | 7M |\n\n**Borderline topics to cover at definition level (30 min total):**\n- Casting defects (blow holes, misrun, shrinkage, warpage) — 10 min\n- Gating system elements (pouring basin, sprue, runner, gate, riser) — 10 min\n- Brazing vs Soldering comparison table — 5 min\n- Ingot/Bloom/Billet/Slab definitions — 5 min\n\n**Total study time: ~12.5 hours**\n**Expected marks: 25-28/30**\n\n### Zero-PYQ Syllabus Topics — Cover at Definition Level (30 min extra):\nThese are IN your syllabus but have NEVER been asked in old papers. The professor could introduce them in the new mid-sem format:\n\n| Topic | What to know | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slush casting | Process + when used (hollow decorative parts) | 5 min |\n| Vacuum casting | Process + advantage (porosity-free) | 5 min |\n| Plaster & ceramic mould casting | Process + applications | 5 min |\n| CO₂ moulding | CO₂ gas + sodium silicate binder → instant hardening | 5 min |\n| Pit & floor moulding | When used (very large castings) | 3 min |\n| Loam sand moulding | What it is (large castings, hand-shaped) | 2 min |\n| Sand testing methods (specific tests) | Grain fineness number, moisture tester, clay washer, permeability meter | 5 min |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Formulas to Memorize\n\n### Rolling\n- **Length of deformation zone:** $l = \\sqrt{R \\cdot \\Delta t}$ where R = roll radius, Δt = draft (reduction in thickness)\n- **Draft:** $\\Delta t = t_i - t_f$ (initial thickness − final thickness)\n- **Maximum draft:** $\\Delta t_{max} = \\mu^2 \\cdot R$ where μ = coefficient of friction\n- **Forward slip:** $S_f = \\frac{v_f - v_r}{v_r}$ where $v_f$ = exit velocity, $v_r$ = roll surface velocity\n- **Roll force:** $F = L \\cdot w \\cdot \\sigma_{avg}$ where L = contact length, w = width\n\n### Wire/Tube Drawing\n- **Drawing stress:** Related to flow stress × ln(A₀/Aₓ) — know the concept, not full derivation\n- **Reduction ratio:** $r = \\frac{A_0 - A_f}{A_0}$\n\n### Pattern Allowances (values to memorize)\n| Material | Shrinkage Allowance (mm/m) |\n|---|---|\n| Cast iron | 10 |\n| Steel | 20 |\n| Aluminium | 13 |\n| Brass | 15 |\n\n- **Draft allowance:** 1° to 3° (external), 2° to 5° (internal)\n- **Machining allowance:** 1.5 to 6 mm depending on size and metal\n\n### Extrusion\n- **Extrusion ratio:** $R = \\frac{A_0}{A_f}$\n\n---\n\n## 7. Chapter-Wise Mark Distribution Prediction (30-Mark Mid-Sem)\n\nBased on GTU patterns for 3-chapter mid-sems:\n\n| Chapter | Expected Marks | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch1 — Foundry Technology | **10-12M** | Largest chapter by content volume. Patterns + casting types + moulding sand. |\n| Ch2 — Metal Joining | **10-12M** | Medium chapter. Gas welding + arc welding + TIG/MIG + defects. |\n| Ch3 — Forming & Shaping | **8-10M** | Compact chapter but high-frequency topics (hot/cold, rolling, extrusion, drawing). |\n\n**Most likely question structure (30M paper):**\n- 3 questions × 10 marks each (3a + 3b + 3c = 3+4+3 or 3+3+4 per question)\n- OR: 5 questions with choice, ~6M each\n- GTU progressive assessments typically use simple