{"step_index":14,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-07T18:35:12Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":true,"Summary":"Full forensic PYQ analysis for Manufacturing Technology (BE05000311) mid-sem exam. Maps 142 questions from 9 old papers (Winter 2022-2025, Summer 2022-2026) against the new 3-chapter syllabus. Tags every PYQ question as IN/OUT/BORDERLINE. Builds topic frequency tables with Tier 1/2/3 classification. Identifies Danger Zones (resistance welding, forging, sheet metal, plastics, super finishing — all OUT despite high PYQ frequency). Provides two study strategies: Strategy A (20/30 marks, ~6.5 hrs) and Strategy B (25/30 marks, ~12.5 hrs). Includes key formulas, mark distribution prediction, and priority matrix.","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# Manufacturing Technology (BE05000311) — Mid-Sem Analysis (30 Marks)\n\n> **Subject:** Manufacturing Technology | **Code:** BE05000311 (old: 3151912)\n> **Exam:** Progressive Assessment, 30 marks | **Sem V, AY 2026-27**\n> **Chapters in scope:** 3 (Foundry Technology, Metal Joining, Forming & Shaping)\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The old papers are **70-mark end-sem** papers covering the **entire syllabus** (5-6 chapters including Plastics, Super Finishing, Forging, Sheet Metal). Your new mid-sem covers **only 3 chapters**. This means:\n> 1. In old papers, your 3 chapters shared space with 2-3 other chapters — each got ~35-45 marks\n> 2. In your 30-mark mid-sem, ALL 30 marks come from these 3 chapters — concentration per topic is **higher**\n> 3. Topics that were 3M in end-sems could become 7M in mid-sems\n\n---\n\n## 1. Syllabus Boundary Verification\n\n### Chapter 1 — Foundry Technology (CO-1)\n\n**✅ IN SCOPE (study these):**\n| Sub-topic | Explicitly named in syllabus? |\n|---|---|\n| Types of patterns + sketches | Yes — \"Types of patterns\" |\n| Pattern allowances (shrinkage, machining, draft, shake, distortion) | Yes — \"pattern allowances\" |\n| Materials used for patterns | Yes — \"materials used for patterns\" |\n| Moulding sand properties | Yes — \"properties of moulding sand\" |\n| Sand testing (grain fineness, moisture, clay, permeability) | Yes — \"sand testing methods\" |\n| Core materials, core making, core prints, core boxes, chaplets | Yes — explicitly listed |\n| Green sand, dry sand, loam sand moulding | Yes |\n| Pit and floor moulding | Yes |\n| Shell moulding | Yes |\n| Permanent moulding | Yes |\n| CO₂ moulding | Yes |\n| Sand casting (fundamentals, procedure) | Yes — \"Fundamentals of metal casting; sand casting\" |\n| Shell mould casting | Yes |\n| Plaster and ceramic mould casting | Yes |\n| Investment casting | Yes |\n| Vacuum casting | Yes |\n| Permanent mould casting | Yes |\n| Slush casting | Yes |\n\n**⚠️ BORDERLINE (know basics, don't deep-dive):**\n| Sub-topic | Why borderline? |\n|---|---|\n| Casting defects (blow holes, misrun, shrinkage, warpage) | Not explicitly listed, but \"Fundamentals of metal casting\" could include them. Asked in 5/9 papers. Know the basics. |\n| Gating system (elements, types, down sprue) | Not named explicitly, but integral to sand casting fundamentals. Asked in 5/9 papers. Know the elements. |\n| Die casting | Syllabus says \"permanent mould casting\" — die casting IS a type. Know it briefly. |\n| Ingot / Bloom / Billet / Slab | Not named in Ch1 but connected to rolling (Ch3). Asked 4/9 times. |\n\n**❌ DANGER ZONES — OUT OF SCOPE (do NOT study these for foundry):**\n| Topic | Why it's a trap | Papers it appeared in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cupola furnace | Melting equipment, not listed | S2026, S2022 |\n| Centrifugal casting | Not listed in casting types | S2026, S2023 |\n| Chvorinov's rule / freezing ratio / riser design | Solidification analysis, not listed | W2023, W2022 |\n| Manufacturing process classification (general) | Old syllabus had this as intro chapter, your new syllabus starts directly at Foundry | W2025, W2024, W2023, W2022, S2026, S2025, S2024, S2023, S2022 |\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 2 — Metal Joining Processes (CO-2)\n\n**✅ IN SCOPE:**\n| Sub-topic | Explicitly named? |\n|---|---|\n| Principles of welding | Yes |\n| Classification of welding and allied processes | Yes |\n| Gas welding (oxy-acetylene process, equipment) | Yes |\n| Gas cutting | Yes |\n| Types of gas flames (neutral, oxidizing, carburizing) | Yes — part of gas welding |\n| Arc welding | Yes |\n| Power sources (AC vs DC) | Yes — \"power sources\" |\n| Arc welding consumables | Yes — \"consumables\" |\n| Polarity (straight/reverse) | Yes — part of power sources |\n| TIG welding | Yes — \"Special welding processes such as TIG\" |\n| MIG welding | Yes — \"MIG welding\" |\n| Welding defects | Yes — \"Welding defects\" |\n| Electrodes and electrode coatings | Yes — explicitly listed |\n| Welding positions (flat, horizontal, vertical, overhead) | Yes — \"welding positions\" |\n\n**⚠️ BORDERLINE:**\n| Sub-topic | Why borderline? |\n|---|---|\n| Brazing vs Soldering | \"Classification of welding and **allied processes**\" could include this as a classification-level question. Asked 5/9 papers. Know the basic comparison table. |\n| Welding joints (butt, lap, T, corner, edge) | Syllabus says \"welding positions\" not \"welding joints.\" Different things. But joints are fundamental welding knowledge. |\n| Arc blow | Part of arc welding phenomena. Not named but implied. |\n| Oxy-hydrogen welding | Syllabus names \"oxy-acetylene\" specifically, not oxy-hydrogen. |\n\n**❌ DANGER ZONES — OUT OF SCOPE:**\n| Topic | Why it's a trap | Papers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resistance welding (Spot, Seam) | **NOT listed.** Syllabus names gas, arc, TIG, MIG only. | W2025, W2024, W2023, W2022, S2022 — **5 papers!** |\n| Laser beam welding | Not listed | W2024, S2026, S2024 |\n| Plasma welding | Not listed | S2024, W2022 |\n| Electron beam welding | Not listed | S2026 |\n| Thermit welding | Not listed | S2023 |\n| Submerged arc welding | Not listed (SAW is NOT the same as general arc welding) | S2026 |\n| Explosive welding | Not listed | S2024 |\n| Leftward/rightward welding techniques | Very specific gas welding technique, not explicitly named | W2025 |\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Resistance welding is the #1 trap.** It appears in 5 out of 9 papers, always for 4-7 marks. It LOOKS like it belongs in the welding chapter. But your new syllabus does NOT list it. Spot welding, seam welding, resistance welding cycle — all ❌ OUT.\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 3 — Forming and Shaping Processes (CO-3)\n\n**✅ IN SCOPE:**\n| Sub-topic | Explicitly named? |\n|---|---|\n| Metal working | Yes |\n| Elastic and plastic deformation | Yes |\n| Hot and cold working (comparison, advantages/disadvantages) | Yes |\n| Strain hardening (concept, effects) | Yes |\n| Rolling — principle and operations | Yes |\n| Rolling — roll pass sequence | Yes — \"roll pass sequence\" |\n| Rolling mill arrangements/types | Yes — part of \"operations\" |\n| Extrusion (forward/backward, direct/indirect) | Yes — \"Extrusion\" |\n| Wire drawing | Yes — \"wire and tube drawing processes\" |\n| Tube drawing | Yes |\n\n**⚠️ BORDERLINE:**\n| Sub-topic | Why borderline? |\n|---|---|\n| Ingot / Bloom / Billet / Slab definitions | Not named but they're the raw material chain for rolling. Know definitions. |\n| Recrystallization temperature | Not named, but defines the boundary between hot and cold working. Fundamental to understanding both. |\n| Rolling formula: l = √(RΔt) | \"Principle and operations\" could include this derivation. Asked once (S2024). |\n| Forward slip, backward slip, neutral point | \"Principle and operations\" — these are rolling parameters. Asked once (S2022). |\n| Seamless tube production | Related to tube drawing/forming but specific process (Mannesmann) not named. |\n\n**❌ DANGER ZONES — OUT OF SCOPE:**\n| Topic | Why it's a trap | Papers |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Forging (ALL types)** | Drop forging, smith forging, machine forging, closed die, open die, forging operations (coining, heading, punching), forging defects, forging presses — **NONE of this is in your syllabus** | W2025, W2024, W2023, S2026, S2025, S2024, S2023 — **7 papers!** |\n| **Sheet metal operations** | Blanking, piercing, shearing, deep drawing, bending, notching, spring back, stamping, compound/progressive die — **ALL OUT** | W2025, W2024, W2023, S2026, S2025, S2024, S2023, S2022 — **8 papers!** |\n| **Plastics (ALL)** | Thermoplastics, thermosetting, injection moulding, blow moulding, compression moulding, calendering, thermoforming, plastic properties — **ALL OUT** | Every single paper |\n| **Super finishing (ALL)** | Lapping, honing, burnishing, buffing, polishing, barrel tumbling, powder coating — **ALL OUT** | Every single paper |\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **Forging and Sheet Metal are the BIGGEST time-wasters.** They appear in 7-8 out of 9 papers, often for 7-mark questions. Students who study from old PYQs without checking the new syllabus will burn 4-6 hours on topics worth exactly **zero marks** on your mid-sem.\n\n---\n\n## 2. PYQ Papers Used\n\n| # | Paper | Season | Year | Code | Questions Analyzed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Winter 2025 | Winter | 2025 | 3151912 | 14 (incl. OR) |\n| 2 | Winter 2024 | Winter | 2024 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 3 | Winter 2023 | Winter | 2023 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 4 | Winter 2022 | Winter | 2022 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 5 | Summer 2026 | Summer | 2026 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 6 | Summer 2025 | Summer | 2025 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 7 | Summer 2024 | Summer | 2024 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 8 | Summer 2023 | Summer | 2023 | 3151912 | 16 |\n| 9 | Summer 2022 | Summer | 2022 | 3151912 | 16 |\n\n**Total questions analyzed: ~142**\n**Questions IN scope: ~65 (~46%)**\n**Questions OUT of scope: ~60 (~42%)**\n**Questions BORDERLINE: ~17 (~12%)**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Only ~46% of old PYQ questions are relevant to your new mid-sem. More than half the old papers are waste for your exam. This is why blind PYQ grinding without syllabus mapping is dangerous.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Topic Frequency Analysis\n\n### Chapter 1 — Foundry Technology (CO-1)\n\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 9) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|:---:|\n| Types of patterns (with sketches) | **7** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Pattern allowances | **6** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Investment casting (full process) | **5** | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Sand casting (procedure/fundamentals) | 4 | 3-4M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Properties of moulding sand | 3 | 4-7M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Core types / core making | 3 | 4M | Theory + Diagram | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Shell mould casting | 2 | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| ⚠️ Casting defects | 5 | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Gating system | 5 | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | ⚠️ Borderline |\n\n**Syllabus topics with ZERO PYQ history:**\n- Slush casting, vacuum casting, plaster & ceramic mould casting, CO₂ moulding, pit & floor moulding, loam sand moulding, sand testing methods (grain fineness/moisture/clay/permeability)\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Zero PYQ history does NOT mean safe to skip.** These are explicitly in YOUR syllabus. The old papers never tested them because the old syllabus may have structured them differently. For a new-format mid-sem, the professor could pull from ANY in-scope topic. These \"virgin\" topics are unpredictable — cover them at definition level minimum.\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 2 — Metal Joining Processes (CO-2)\n\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 9) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|:---:|\n| Gas welding / oxy-acetylene / 3 flame types | **8** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| TIG vs MIG comparison | 4 | 4M | Comparison table | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Electrode coatings / purpose | 4 | 3-4M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Polarity / power sources (AC vs DC) | 4 | 3-4M | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Welding defects (causes & remedies) | 3 | 4-7M | Theory + Table | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Classification of welding processes | 3 | 3-4M | Theory + Chart | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| MIG welding (detailed with sketch) | 2 | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| TIG welding (detailed with sketch) | 1 | 7M | Theory + Diagram | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| Electrode coding system | 1 | 4M | Theory | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| ⚠️ Brazing vs Soldering | 5 | 3M | Comparison | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Welding joints (butt, lap, T, etc.) | 2 | 4M | Theory + Diagram | ⚠️ Borderline |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Gas welding / oxy-acetylene / flame types is the SINGLE MOST ASKED TOPIC across all 9 papers — 8 out of 9.** If you study one thing from Chapter 2, this is it. Know the 3 flame types (neutral, oxidizing, carburizing), their temperature zones, which metals each is used for, and the oxy-acetylene equipment setup. Draw all 3 from memory.\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 3 — Forming and Shaping Processes (CO-3)\n\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 9) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|:---:|\n| Extrusion (forward vs backward) | **7** | 4-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Hot working vs Cold working | **6** | 3-7M | Comparison table | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Rolling (principle, operations, mill types) | **6** | 3-7M | Theory + Diagram | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Wire drawing vs Tube drawing | **5** | 4-7M | Theory + Diagram + Comparison | 🔴 **Tier 1** |\n| Strain hardening (effects, concept) | 2 | 3M | Theory | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n| ⚠️ Ingot / Bloom / Billet / Slab definitions | 4 | 3M | Definitions | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Rolling formula l = √(RΔt) | 1 | 4M | Derivation | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Forward/backward slip, neutral point | 1 | 3M | Theory | ⚠️ Borderline |\n| ⚠️ Recrystallization temperature | 1 | 4M | Theory | ⚠️ Borderline |\n\n---\n\n## 4. 🎯 Strategy A: 20/30 Marks (~6-8 hours)\n\nStudy these in this exact order. Tier 1 topics only. If you run out of time, stop — you've covered the highest-probability material.\n\n| # | Topic | Ch | Est. Time | What to learn | Expected marks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Gas welding + 3 flame types | 2 | 1 hr | Oxy-acetylene setup diagram, neutral/oxidizing/carburizing flame sketches + which metals each welds. Gas cutting process. | 3-7M |\n| 2 | Types of patterns | 1 | 30 min | All pattern types (solid, split, match plate, cope & drag, gated, sweep, skeleton, follow board, loose piece). Sketch each. | 3-4M |\n| 3 | Pattern allowances | 1 | 45 min | Shrinkage, machining, draft, shake, distortion allowances. What each is, why needed. | 3-7M |\n| 4 | Hot working vs Cold working | 3 | 30 min | Comparison table: temperature, grain size, surface finish, strength, ductility, residual stress, dimensional accuracy. | 3-7M |\n| 5 | Extrusion (forward vs backward) | 3 | 45 min | Forward extrusion sketch + process. Backward extrusion sketch + process. Comparison table. Hydrostatic extrusion (brief). | 4-7M |\n| 6 | Investment casting | 1 | 30 min | Full step-by-step process with sketch (wax pattern → ceramic shell → dewaxing → pouring → knockout). Advantages/applications. | 7M |\n| 7 | Wire drawing vs Tube drawing | 3 | 45 min | Both processes with sketches. Die angles, reduction ratio. Comparison table. | 4-7M |\n| 8 | Rolling (principle + mills + roll pass) | 3 | 1.5 hr | Rolling principle sketch, types of mills (2-high, 3-high, 4-high, cluster, tandem). Roll pass sequence. Products (ingot → bloom → billet → slab). | 4-7M |\n\n**Total study time: ~6.5 hours**\n**Expected marks: 20-24/30**\n\n### DO NOT WASTE TIME ON (from old PYQs):\n\n| ❌ Topic | Why students waste time on it | Old PYQ frequency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resistance welding (Spot/Seam) | Looks like it belongs in welding chapter | 5/9 papers |\n| Forging (all types + operations) | Heavy PYQ history, NOT in your syllabus | 7/9 papers |\n| Sheet metal (blanking/piercing/shearing) | Very frequently asked | 8/9 papers |\n| Injection moulding / Plastics | In almost every paper | 9/9 papers |\n| Super finishing (lapping/honing/burnishing) | In almost every paper | 8/9 papers |\n| Laser/Plasma/Electron beam welding | Looks like welding chapter content | 4/9 papers |\n| Manufacturing process classification | Was Q1 in most old papers | 7/9 papers |\n| Chvorinov's rule / Riser design | Looks like casting topic | 3/9 papers |\n| Cupola furnace | Looks like foundry topic | 2/9 papers |\n\n---\n\n## 5. 🎯 Strategy B: 25/30 Marks (~12-15 hours)\n\nEverything from Strategy A (6.5 hours), PLUS:\n\n| # | Topic | Ch | Est. Time | Why study | Expected marks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 9 | TIG vs MIG comparison | 2 | 30 min | 4/9 papers. Process differences, shielding gas, filler wire, applications, current type. | 4M |\n| 10 | Welding defects (causes + remedies) | 2 | 45 min | 3/9 papers. Porosity, undercut, spatter, incomplete fusion, slag inclusion, cracking. Cause-remedy table. | 4-7M |\n| 11 | Electrode coatings + purpose | 2 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Quick-win 3M question. Functions: arc stability, gas shielding, slag formation, alloying. | 3M |\n| 12 | Polarity (straight/reverse) + AC vs DC | 2 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Quick-win 3M question. Which polarity for thick/thin work, heat distribution. | 3-4M |\n| 13 | Moulding sand properties | 1 | 30 min | 3/9 papers. Permeability, flowability, collapsibility, cohesiveness, refractoriness, green strength. | 4-7M |\n| 14 | Core types / core making | 1 | 30 min | 3/9 papers. Types of cores (horizontal, vertical, balanced, hanging), core prints, chaplets. | 4M |\n| 15 | Sand casting step-by-step procedure | 1 | 20 min | 4/9 papers. Pattern → moulding → core setting → closing → pouring → solidification → shakeout → cleaning. | 3M |\n| 16 | Classification of welding processes | 2 | 20 min | 3/9 papers. Draw the classification tree (fusion vs pressure, gas vs arc vs resistance, etc.) | 3-4M |\n| 17 | Strain hardening (concept + effects) | 3 | 15 min | 2/9 papers. What happens to strength/ductility/hardness. Stress-strain curve changes. | 3M |\n| 18 | Shell mould casting | 1 | 25 min | 2/9 papers. Full process with sketch. Thermosetting resin bonded sand + heated metal pattern. | 7M |\n| 19 | MIG welding — full process sketch | 2 | 25 min | 2/9 papers but explicitly in syllabus. Setup sketch + consumable electrode + inert gas. | 7M |\n| 20 | TIG welding — full process sketch | 2 | 25 min | 1/9 papers but explicitly in syllabus. Setup sketch + non-consumable tungsten electrode + inert gas. | 7M |\n\n**Borderline topics to cover at definition level (30 min total):**\n- Casting defects (blow holes, misrun, shrinkage, warpage) — 10 min\n- Gating system elements (pouring basin, sprue, runner, gate, riser) — 10 min\n- Brazing vs Soldering comparison table — 5 min\n- Ingot/Bloom/Billet/Slab definitions — 5 min\n\n**Total study time: ~12.5 hours**\n**Expected marks: 25-28/30**\n\n### Zero-PYQ Syllabus Topics — Cover at Definition Level (30 min extra):\nThese are IN your syllabus but have NEVER been asked in old papers. The professor could introduce them in the new mid-sem format:\n\n| Topic | What to know | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slush casting | Process (pour out unhardened metal) + when used (hollow decorative parts) | 5 min |\n| Vacuum casting | Process + advantage (porosity-free castings) | 5 min |\n| Plaster & ceramic mould casting | Process + applications (intricate shapes, low melting alloys) | 5 min |\n| CO₂ moulding | CO₂ gas + sodium silicate binder → instant hardening of mould | 5 min |\n| Pit & floor moulding | When used (very large castings that don't fit in flask) | 3 min |\n| Loam sand moulding | What it is (large castings, hand-shaped with loam sand) | 2 min |\n| Sand testing methods | Grain fineness number test, moisture tester, clay content (clay washer), permeability test | 5 min |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Formulas to Memorize\n\n### Rolling\n- **Length of deformation zone:** $l = \\sqrt{R \\cdot \\Delta t}$ where R = roll radius, Δt = draft\n- **Draft:** $\\Delta t = t_i - t_f$ (initial − final thickness)\n- **Maximum draft:** $\\Delta t_{max} = \\mu^2 \\cdot R$ where μ = coefficient of friction\n- **Forward slip:** $S_f = \\frac{v_f - v_r}{v_r}$ where $v_f$ = exit velocity, $v_r$ = roll surface velocity\n\n### Wire/Tube Drawing\n- **Reduction ratio:** $r = \\frac{A_0 - A_f}{A_0}$\n\n### Pattern Allowances (values to memorize)\n| Material | Shrinkage Allowance (mm/m) |\n|---|---|\n| Cast iron | 10 |\n| Steel | 20 |\n| Aluminium | 13 |\n| Brass | 15 |\n\n- **Draft allowance:** 1° to 3° (external), 2° to 5° (internal)\n- **Machining allowance:** 1.5 to 6 mm depending on size and metal\n\n### Extrusion\n- **Extrusion ratio:** $R_e = \\frac{A_0}{A_f}$\n\n---\n\n## 7. Chapter-Wise Mark Distribution Prediction (30-Mark Mid-Sem)\n\nBased on GTU progressive assessment patterns for 3-chapter mid-sems:\n\n| Chapter | Expected Marks | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch1 — Foundry Technology | **10-12M** | Largest chapter by content volume (patterns + casting types + moulding sand + cores). |\n| Ch2 — Metal Joining | **10-12M** | Medium chapter (gas welding + arc welding + TIG/MIG + defects + electrodes). |\n| Ch3 — Forming & Shaping | **8-10M** | Compact chapter but high-frequency topics (hot/cold working, rolling, extrusion, drawing). |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Priority Matrix — Quick Reference\n\n```\n                    HIGH PYQ FREQUENCY\n                          │\n    ┌─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐\n    │                     │                     │\n    │   ⚠️ BORDERLINE      │   🔴 TIER 1           │\n    │   BUT STUDY          │   MUST STUDY          │\n    │                     │                     │\n    │   • Casting defects  │   • Gas welding +     │\n    │   • Gating system    │     3 flames (8/9)    │\n    │   • Brazing vs       │   • Extrusion (7/9)   │\n    │     Soldering        │   • Types of          │\n    │   • Ingot/Bloom/     │     patterns (7/9)    │\n    │     Billet defs      │   • Pattern           │\n    │                     │     allowances (6/9)  │\nNOT IN ──────────────────┼────────────────── IN  │\nSYLLABUS                 │                SYLLABUS│\n    │                     │                     │\n    │   ❌ DANGER ZONE      │   🟡 TIER 2 / 🟢 3    │\n    │   DO NOT STUDY       │   STUDY IF TIME      │\n    │                     │                     │\n    │   • Forging (7/9)    │   • TIG vs MIG (4/9)  │\n    │   • Sheet metal (8/9)│   • Electrode         │\n    │   • Plastics (9/9)   │     coatings (4/9)    │\n    │   • Super finish(8/9)│   • Welding           │\n    │   • Resistance       │     defects (3/9)     │\n    │     welding (5/9)    │   • Strain             │\n    │                     │     hardening (2/9)   │\n    └─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┘\n                          │\n                    LOW PYQ FREQUENCY\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Coaching Transition\n\n### ✅ Analysis Complete — Ready to Start Coaching?\n\nYour study map is above. Now we move into active recall mode.\n\n**If you have your coaching prompt file**, say:\n> \"Start coaching. Prompt file: [path to your gtu_midsem_prompt.md]\"\n\nI will read the coaching prompt, adopt that role, and begin drilling you using the study priorities above — starting with Tier 1 topics in the recommended order.\n\n**If you don't have the coaching prompt**, I'll default to Mode 2 (Deep practice) — one GTU-format question at a time, graded with specific point breakdowns.\n\nWhich topic do you want to start with?\n","Description":"Full forensic PYQ analysis for Manufacturing Technology mid-sem, mapping 9 old papers against the new 3-chapter syllabus with tier classification, danger zones, and two study strategies.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\cc5ae5f0-3c52-483a-b0d6-e323018f022c\\BE05000311_midsem_analysis.md","toolAction":"Creating analysis report","toolSummary":"Midsem analysis artifact"}}]}
